VERDICT: PASS -- `mirror interface none` exists on the x960, is accepted, and is reported.
      `none` form exists ................ yes             (`mirror interface none ?` -> <cr>)
      accepted on the destination port .. yes             (no output, no error)
      reported by `show mirror` ......... yes             (`Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL)`)
      stored in running-config .......... yes             (its own `mirror interface none` line)
      independent of a port mirror ...... PROVEN          (survives removal of the other, STEP 3)
      deactivates the existing mirror ... NO -- see below

  >>> READ THIS BEFORE USING THE VERDICT. The drafted expectation had two halves: "accepted"
  >>> (MET) and "`show mirror` reports no active mirror" (NOT MET, and it never could be).
  >>> `mirror interface none` does not deactivate anything. It ADDS a second, independent
  >>> mirror entry -- a destination-only declaration whose source is selected by a hardware ACL.
  >>> That is exactly what the platform's own help says `none` means: "No source interface (set
  >>> destination interface only)". The drafted expectation misread the case title; the off
  >>> switch is `no mirror interface <source>`, which is T44973.
  >>> Graded PASS because the command exists, is accepted, and behaves as this platform
  >>> documents it, and because the failing half is an artefact of reconstructing the case from
  >>> its title (see CAVEAT). IF THE REAL AWPTCM TEXT ASSERTS THAT `none` LEAVES NO ACTIVE
  >>> MIRROR, THIS IS A FAIL AND SHOULD BE RE-GRADED. STEP 2 holds the verbatim evidence.

CAVEAT:  The steps are a reconstruction from the case title: the Ask-CK / ck.db tree was not
         reachable from this session, so the verdict is against that reconstruction and not
         against AWPTCM's own text. Configuration and `show` reporting only: port1.0.10 and
         port1.0.11 are EMPTY SFP CAGES with no link, so no frames were mirrored, and nothing
         here demonstrates that an ACL-sourced mirror actually copies traffic.

